# The turns of one loop are laid out several times in a row: once for each kind of a view, or for each pair of kinds of storage. Each layout ended in the front of the next: so a run of fewer turns than a layout holds,
# and the rest behind the turns laid out, were asked "are four left" once a layout: eight times for a view. All layouts of a row hold the same number of turns: too few for one are too few for all: they go straight to the
# loop as written. Only a failed question for the kind, and a turn that is left, lead to the next front. A call over 4 doubles 181 > 155 instructions, over 12 Int16 252 > 235; no kernel of 48 slower by more than x1.002.
# Eight layouts: the rest of seven passes the others by.
# RUN: compile UnrolledLoopRemaindersShared mechanisms=all
# CHECK: statistic UnrolledLoopRemaindersShared 7
# No turn over a view is ever left for the next front (no hole, no store that changes a kind): a front is come to from the front before it alone, where the count was asked. So the first front asks it for all eight.
# RUN(ONCE): compile UnrolledLoopTripChecksReused mechanisms=all
# ONCE: statistic UnrolledLoopTripChecksReused 7
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell mechanisms=all
